1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:22,060
is nothing more familiar, more mysterious, more breathtaking in its action, marvelous in its mechanics,

2
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:30,820
exquisite in its range of senses, and staggering in its ability to understand.

3
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:40,610
On a fantastic voyage through a single day, we plunge deep into the routine miracles of the human body.

4
00:00:40,610 --> 00:00:50,840
Our instruments, engines, infrastructure, roadways and circuitry,

5
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:59,600
through 10,000 blinks, 20,000 breaths, 100,000 beats.

6
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,600
Hello.

7
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:08,600
Today is an ordinary, extraordinary day in the life of the incredible human machine.

8
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:26,460
The dust is really all we are.

9
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:35,830
Oxygen, carbon, hydrogen, and a handful of elements that would cost very little at any chemical supply shop.

10
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:38,530
But get these chemicals together.

11
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:47,530
these chemicals together, marinate in a hospitable place for about 3.8 billion years, and the mundane mix of molecules becomes precious.

12
00:01:47,530 --> 00:02:01,370
There are more than 6 billion human bodies living on Earth, and each of us is the amalgamation of some 100 trillion microscopic cells.

13
00:02:01,370 --> 00:02:08,370
While the blueprint for each individual are 99.9% identical, no two of us are exactly the same.

14
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:16,670
New Day dawns, each human machine begins the succession of

15
00:02:16,690 --> 00:02:19,690
miracles that will take it from morning to midnight.

16
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:39,090
Muscles, bones, hearts, brains, all must marshal their forces and unite, just to wake us up.

17
00:02:39,090 --> 00:03:04,420
...service of it all, a velvety overcoat of cells and protein keeps us in, and the rest of the world out.

18
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:15,220
Our radiator, our entreat of pain and pleasure, it is the body's largest organ, our skin.

19
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:22,040
Our skin, smooth and silky to the eyes and touch.

20
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:26,040
A closer look presents a very different landscape.

21
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:31,520
Magnified 600 times, our outermost skin is nothing but dead cells,

22
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:36,520
riddled with ridges and grooves, and pocked with countless bumps and holes.

23
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:44,990
Look closer still, and we find hundreds of thousands of bacteria inhabit every square inch of us.

24
00:03:44,990 --> 00:03:58,620
With every tick of the clock, our dead skin gets sloughed off.

25
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:08,160
off. We shed at least 600,000 particles of skin an hour, about a pound and a half's worth each year,

26
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:12,160
which accounts for as much as 80% of the dust in our houses.

27
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:25,180
But there's plenty of skin to go around.

28
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:33,970
If we could peel it off and lay it out flat, the average person's skin would cover some 18 square feet.

29
00:04:33,970 --> 00:04:39,820
Though just millimeters thick, it would weigh about six pounds.

30
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:42,820
And we're constantly making more.

31
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:48,080
Just wait a month or so, and you'll have a shiny new coat,

32
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:53,130
which means skin can't be all dead.

33
00:04:53,130 --> 00:05:02,010
Dip below the surface, and you find cells continuously dividing to replace those dead cells above.

34
00:05:02,010 --> 00:05:10,210
Kilometers of blood vessels pulse to skin's connective tissues.

35
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:15,350
Not forgetting all those precious nerve endings.

36
00:05:15,350 --> 00:05:26,050
45 miles worth of nerve stretch from our heads down to our toes,

37
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,050
and many reach to our skin.

38
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:32,120
Some people.

39
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:40,750
parts more than others. If sensitivity were size dependent, we would look something like this.

40
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:53,180
Our super sensitive hands, feet, tongue and lips, each packed with touch receptors, would swell enormously.

41
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:56,180
Our legs on the other hand would resemble a chickens.

42
00:05:56,180 --> 00:06:07,970
Good thing for us, there is more to this organ than touch. Skin is also our heating and cooling system.

43
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:09,830
And by helping,

44
00:06:09,830 --> 00:06:14,830
Maintain that comfy 98.6 degrees Fahrenheit, it keeps us alive.

45
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:24,460
Through its network of blood vessels, skin carries as much as one-third of the heart's hot, freshly pumped blood.

46
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:31,480
Get too hot, and these vessels can widen to release heat from our bodies.

47
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,180
But sometimes that's not enough.

48
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:46,830
A good workout can raise the body's temperature several degrees above normal.

49
00:06:46,830 --> 00:06:50,830
A potentially deadly state if it weren't for skin's slick safety net.

50
00:06:50,830 --> 00:07:00,360
Sweat. More than 2 million holes across our skin can produce up to half a gallon of the liquid in an hour.

51
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:10,290
The heated droplets evaporate into the air and leave the body cool.

52
00:07:10,290 --> 00:07:16,490
A special camera that images heat, not light.

53
00:07:16,490 --> 00:07:19,490
We can see this air conditioning system at work.

54
00:07:19,490 --> 00:07:23,960
The hotter the body, the deeper the red.

55
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:33,480
No surprise, some of the warmest parts of our bodies, our foreheads, palms and armpits,

56
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:35,480
correlate with the greatest concentration of sweat glass.

57
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:37,480
of sweat glands.

58
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:48,580
We're more likely to encounter the opposite extreme in our morning routine.

59
00:07:48,580 --> 00:08:00,060
Supposed to a chill, tiny muscles jerk our hairs to attention, bulging the skin around them.

60
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:10,700
Goose bumps.

61
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:16,700
Scientists have dubbed us the naked ape, but look closely, and we find were anything but naked.

62
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:28,610
Some of our skin cells form tubes that produce hair, and we have as many of these follicles punctuating our skin as do our hairy relatives.

63
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:31,610
some 5 million of them.

64
00:08:31,610 --> 00:08:37,740
Few hair cells divide.

65
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:40,740
They push old ones up and out.

66
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:45,020
By the time our hair breaches the surface, it's dead,

67
00:08:45,020 --> 00:08:49,590
which is why we don't feel pain with every haircut.

68
00:08:49,590 --> 00:08:57,460
Cleaned and groomed, we venture into the bustling morning world.

69
00:08:57,460 --> 00:09:05,180
Around us, other incredible human machines greet the day

70
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:08,180
in all different sizes and shapes, colors and textures.

71
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:12,970
But on some level, we all inhabit the same skin.

72
00:09:12,970 --> 00:09:17,020
the same skin.

73
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:21,020
Below the surface, each of us has about the same number of melanocytes,

74
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:27,020
the cells that pump the dark pigment melanin, into our hair and skin, and give them color.

75
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:36,040
It's just the amount of melanin these cells produce that determines whether we are black or white, brunette or blonde.

76
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:42,000
Generally speaking, the more melanin, the darker.

77
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,540
That same chemical adds color to a very different sensory organ.

78
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:52,140
organ, blue or brown, green or hazel.

79
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:57,140
They are the most vital sensory organ that the incredible machine has.

80
00:09:57,140 --> 00:10:04,320
Our day has just begun, and the magnificent miracle of sight leads the way.

81
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:21,250
In microseconds, our eyes sight, follow, focus, and process images fractions of an inch long,

82
00:10:21,250 --> 00:10:26,250
or moving at hundreds of miles per hour, enabling us to assess and appreciate the world

83
00:10:26,250 --> 00:10:28,250
more than any other sense.

84
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:42,560
They may be the windows to our souls, but on a less poetic level,

85
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:48,980
eyes are just hungry harvesters of light, trapping and translating it into electrical impulses

86
